WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.950
So, the most damaging bias that can come up
in poker, and I also think very often in life,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.660
is what's known as confirmation bias.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:22.410
So that's basically when you have like a pre-existing
want to believe something, a desire for something

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:30.230
to be true, and you will overvalue evidence
that confirms that belief and disvalue evidence

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.280
that disproves it.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.590
And in poker that can be absolutely devastating
if, say, you're in a big hand and a ton of

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:44.140
your chips are in the middle and your opponent
has made a big bet and your hand is not that

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.260
strong, and you really, really want to believe
that they're bluffing so that you can win

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.700
this hand.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.690
And you'll then look for all these signs:
“Oh, well, I saw that their hand look shaky

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:58.280
in that way and they seem a little uncertain,”
look for all the evidence that could suggest

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.160
that they are bluffing, and then not really
ask yourself those vital questions like, “Okay

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.720
well what evidence is there that they actually
have a strong hand?”

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.250
And learning that skill is absolutely crucial
to be a great poker player, and I think that

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.470
applies to many paths.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.180
So if you're trying to make a big business
decision: “Should I hire this new person

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.180
as a manager?”

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.549
and they're an old family friend and you really,
really like them so you really do want to

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:32.210
hire them, you'll tend to look for all the
signs that they could be great but dismiss

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:38.000
the perhaps overwhelming signs or the very
strong signs that they're actually not the

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.329
right person for the job.

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:49.010
So it's very important to just remember that
our brains are naturally inclined to confirm

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.720
what we really want to believe.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:59.860
The status quo bias is whenever we catch ourselves
saying, “Well it's always been done this

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:05.050
way” or “It’s worked for me like this
in the past and this is my way of doing things”—that's

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.940
the status quo bias rearing its rather ugly
head.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:18.489
And what this means is that we have a tendency
to not want to change our methodology, perhaps

00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:25.410
change the process with which we do something
because it perhaps worked sometime in the

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.050
past or we like doing it that way.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:35.030
And in poker I remember I used to catch myself
falling into that, I had like a style of play

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:42.250
that had worked for me a number of times and
I would over rely on that and not update my

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.160
playing style based upon the table that I
was at because perhaps I didn't enjoy it as

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.090
much or I found it more scary, for example.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.530
And the same thing can apply to us in life.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:59.580
The important thing there is to just be aware
of the bias and look for situations where

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.530
you could be using it to justify staying in
a situation that you're a little bit scared

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.640
to change.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:12.269
Another classic one that comes up in poker
is the sunk cost fallacy where, again, you'll

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:17.680
have a lot of chips, perhaps almost all of
your stack is in the middle and yet you are

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:26.769
85 to 90 percent confident that you have the
worst hand and that putting another chip in

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:33.690
the pot is probably not a good idea, but we'll
often go to ourselves, “Well I've gone this

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:37.060
far, I've put this much in I might as well
see it through to the end.”

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:42.080
But if you have very strong information that
actually from this point onwards putting more

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.220
money in the pot is a bad idea, then you shouldn't.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.890
But we have this belief that while I've put
this much time in or this much effort that

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.450
we should continue on.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:57.430
In poker whenever a new card comes out, even
though historically you've put in a lot of

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:03.090
money into the pot, when that new information
comes out that's an entirely new situation,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:08.790
what's happened before in terms of how much
money you've put in is actually largely irrelevant.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:17.959
And the same thing applies to if you're investing
in a business, be it time or money, when no

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:22.290
matter how much you've put in over the last
few years, let's say if new information comes

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.949
out that this market is shrinking, some like
legislation has been passed that's going to

00:04:26.949 --> 00:04:34.150
make it even harder to proceed, then you have
to largely ignore the decisions that you made

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.520
yesterday or before you got that information.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:42.440
You're in an entirely new situation and you
have to evaluate it with all the information

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:43.440
that you now have available.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:50.160
Yeah, it's interesting to observe sort of
the difference in emotion you can have when

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.180
you end up in a very similar situation.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:58.780
So for example, I had 100,000 chips, I'm in
a big tournament and I have 100,000 chips

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.960
and I lose a big hand and I go down to 50,000.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:08.430
And then if I compare it to I'm in the same
tournament and I had 25,000 chips, I double

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.990
up my stack and end up with 50,000.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:12.560
So these are two different situations.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:18.770
I have the same number of chips but I feel
completely different between the two.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:24.710
And those differing mindsets—in the time
I've just doubled up I'm feeling great I'm

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:29.560
like “Wow I'm up to here now let's see where
we can go,” whereas I've halved my stack,

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.800
I'm feeling despondent, I was like “Man
I had this many before.”

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.960
And so often you'll end up playing badly from
this point and playing great from this point,

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.789
and yet it's stupid because actually practically
you're in exactly the same situation in both.

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:50.290
So poker teaches you to detach yourself from
how you got there and just going, “Okay

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.320
this is my situation right now, what's the
best procedure moving forward?”

